From: Stoicoci Mihaita <stoicocimihaita@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] q706f: store the RTC offset in PM8150 SDAM2 (0xb1bc..0xb1bf)

*** DO NOT APPLY WITHOUT OWNER APPROVAL ***

rtc-pm8xxx cannot set the PMIC RTC counter, so the clock starts in 1970.
Give it an nvmem "offset" cell, as sc8280xp boards do with PMK8280 SDAM6
byte 0xbc. No upstream SM8150/SM8250 board does this on PM8150, so the
cell location is our choice, not an established one: SDAM2 (0xb100),
bytes 0xbc-0xbf. The vendor kernel only touches 0x42, 0x80-0x94, 0xe5,
0xe6 of that block, and on this unit 0x95-0xbf hold what looks like
never-initialised data, but boot firmware/PBS use of those bytes cannot
be ruled out from the sources we have. The write is persistent while the
PMIC has battery power and is shared with Android on the other slot.

Needs CONFIG_NVMEM_SPMI_SDAM (=m is enough, the RTC probe defers).

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-lenovo-q706f.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-lenovo-q706f.dts
@@ -1317,6 +1317,31 @@
 	status = "disabled";
 };
 
+&pm8150_0 {
+	/*
+	 * The PMIC RTC counter is not written by Linux. Keep the offset to
+	 * wall-clock time in four otherwise unused bytes of PM8150 SDAM2.
+	 * The vendor kernel uses this block for fg-gen4 data (0x80-0x94) and
+	 * PBS triggers for the LED driver (0x42, 0xe5, 0xe6) only.
+	 */
+	rtc@6000 {
+		nvmem-cells = <&rtc_offset>;
+		nvmem-cell-names = "offset";
+	};
+
+	pm8150_sdam_2: nvram@b100 {
+		compatible = "qcom,spmi-sdam";
+		reg = <0xb100>;
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <1>;
+		ranges = <0 0xb100 0x100>;
+
+		rtc_offset: rtc-offset@bc {
+			reg = <0xbc 0x4>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
 &pm8150_gpios {
 	gpio-line-names = "NC", /* GPIO_1 */
 	"NC",
